1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,171
Before a program can sign users in through your Orchard Core site, or

2
00:00:05,171 --> 00:00:07,506
ask it for a token, you register it.

3
00:00:07,506 --> 00:00:12,344
In this video, you create applications, choose their flows and scopes, and define

4
00:00:12,344 --> 00:00:13,790
a scope of your own.

5
00:00:18,390 --> 00:00:23,303
An application is a program that uses your site as its identity provider:

6
00:00:23,303 --> 00:00:27,843
a web store, a mobile app, or a service that runs without a person.

7
00:00:27,843 --> 00:00:31,450
You register each one, and each one has a Client Id.

8
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:35,961
A scope is a named kind of access, such as Email or Roles.

9
00:00:35,961 --> 00:00:40,250
When an application asks for a token, it lists the scopes it wants,

10
00:00:40,250 --> 00:00:44,540
and you choose which scopes each application is allowed to request.

11
00:00:45,290 --> 00:00:49,397
Both are managed by the OpenID Connect Management UI feature.

12
00:00:49,397 --> 00:00:54,974
The OpenID Connect Authorization Server feature depends on it, so enabling the server

13
00:00:54,974 --> 00:01:00,000
adds the Applications and Scopes pages under Access Control, OpenID Connect.

14
00:01:04,223 --> 00:01:08,120
Let's open Access Control, OpenID Connect, Applications.

15
00:01:08,120 --> 00:01:14,092
The page lists each registered application by display name, with Edit and Delete buttons.

16
00:01:14,092 --> 00:01:17,613
New application opens the form that registers one.

17
00:01:18,071 --> 00:01:22,553
Display Name is how the application is listed, so we enter Inventory Sync.

18
00:01:22,553 --> 00:01:24,682
The Type is Confidential client:

19
00:01:24,682 --> 00:01:30,171
it must send its client secret when it communicates with the token and revocation endpoints.

20
00:01:30,698 --> 00:01:36,450
Client Id identifies the application when it asks for a token, so we enter inventory-sync.

21
00:01:36,450 --> 00:01:41,303
A confidential client also needs a Client Secret, and the button beside the

22
00:01:41,303 --> 00:01:44,358
field that generates a new value fills it in.

23
00:01:44,825 --> 00:01:49,711
Under Flows, the form lists only the flows that the authorization server allows.

24
00:01:49,711 --> 00:01:52,268
A separate video covers those settings.

25
00:01:52,268 --> 00:01:56,870
Let's check Allow Client Credentials Flow, which lets a service get a token

26
00:01:56,870 --> 00:01:58,745
for itself, without a user.

27
00:01:59,238 --> 00:02:01,338
Client Credentials Roles appears.

28
00:02:01,338 --> 00:02:05,644
The application receives the roles you check whenever it gets a token this

29
00:02:05,644 --> 00:02:07,258
way, so we check Editor.

30
00:02:07,715 --> 00:02:11,218
Allowed Scopes lists the scopes of the site by name.

31
00:02:11,218 --> 00:02:15,991
The default ones describe a user's data, which this service does not use,

32
00:02:15,991 --> 00:02:18,951
so we leave them unchecked and select Save.

33
00:02:18,951 --> 00:02:22,575
The list shows Inventory Sync beside Legacy Prototype.

34
00:02:26,811 --> 00:02:31,073
Let's select New application again to register a web application.

35
00:02:31,073 --> 00:02:35,636
We enter Storefront Web as the display name, and choose Public client.

36
00:02:35,636 --> 00:02:41,399
The Client Secret field disappears, because public applications don't use a client secret,

37
00:02:41,399 --> 00:02:44,521
and Allow Client Credentials Flow is disabled.

38
00:02:44,976 --> 00:02:47,329
The Client Id is storefront-web.

39
00:02:47,329 --> 00:02:52,408
Let's check Allow Authorization Code Flow, the flow that sends users to your

40
00:02:52,408 --> 00:02:53,771
site to sign in.

41
00:02:53,771 --> 00:02:56,806
Allow Refresh Token Flow becomes available.

42
00:02:57,314 --> 00:03:00,066
More fields appear below the flows.

43
00:03:00,066 --> 00:03:04,765
Redirect Uris lists where the site may send users back to, so we

44
00:03:04,765 --> 00:03:07,316
enter an address on example.com.

45
00:03:07,316 --> 00:03:13,894
Allow End Session Endpoint adds Post-Logout Redirect Uris, where users go after signing out.

46
00:03:14,358 --> 00:03:19,432
Require Proof Key for Code Exchange and Require Pushed Authorization Requests each require

47
00:03:19,432 --> 00:03:21,704
this application to use that feature.

48
00:03:21,704 --> 00:03:25,615
Each hint reminds you to check that your client library supports it.

49
00:03:25,615 --> 00:03:27,148
We check the first one.

50
00:03:27,621 --> 00:03:31,409
Consent type sets how authorization requests are handled.

51
00:03:31,409 --> 00:03:37,123
Explicit consent needs the user's approval, and the hint calls it the recommended option.

52
00:03:37,123 --> 00:03:42,595
Implicit consent shows no consent form, and External consent rejects a request unless

53
00:03:42,595 --> 00:03:44,881
an authorization already exists.

54
00:03:45,354 --> 00:03:52,046
Under Allowed Scopes, we check email and profile, which describe the signed-in user, and select Save.

55
00:03:52,046 --> 00:03:56,174
The list shows Storefront Web beside the other applications.

56
00:04:00,413 --> 00:04:04,154
As the list grows, Search narrows it by display name.

57
00:04:04,154 --> 00:04:09,543
We type Legacy and select the search button, and only Legacy Prototype remains.

58
00:04:09,543 --> 00:04:11,953
Clear brings back the full list.

59
00:04:12,408 --> 00:04:15,673
Edit opens the same form with the saved values.

60
00:04:15,673 --> 00:04:18,754
The Client Secret field is empty on purpose:

61
00:04:18,754 --> 00:04:23,437
enter a new value to replace the secret, or leave it empty to keep it.

62
00:04:23,437 --> 00:04:28,798
This page also offers Allow Introspection Endpoint and Allow Revocation Endpoint.

63
00:04:29,277 --> 00:04:34,474
On the list, Delete asks you to confirm that you want to remove the element.

64
00:04:34,474 --> 00:04:38,657
We select Ok, and Legacy Prototype is removed from the list.

65
00:04:42,892 --> 00:04:46,480
Let's open Access Control, OpenID Connect, Scopes.

66
00:04:46,480 --> 00:04:53,015
On a site with the authorization server enabled, the list holds Email, Phone, Profile and Roles.

67
00:04:53,015 --> 00:04:58,012
Each row shows the display name, the name in a badge, and a description.

68
00:04:58,464 --> 00:05:00,313
Let's select New scope.

69
00:05:00,313 --> 00:05:04,648
Display Name is how the scope is listed, and Name is the value

70
00:05:04,648 --> 00:05:07,964
an application requests, so it must be unique.

71
00:05:07,964 --> 00:05:13,894
We enter Inventory as the display name, inventory as the name, and a short description.

72
00:05:14,359 --> 00:05:19,960
Additional resources (API audiences) names the other APIs that tokens for this scope

73
00:05:19,960 --> 00:05:22,511
are meant for, separated by spaces.

74
00:05:22,511 --> 00:05:26,494
The hint under the field shows how to name another tenant.

75
00:05:26,494 --> 00:05:28,299
We enter inventory-api.

76
00:05:28,751 --> 00:05:32,707
The site itself is always an audience, so you never list it.

77
00:05:32,707 --> 00:05:37,561
We select Save, and Inventory appears in the list with its name in a badge.

78
00:05:38,017 --> 00:05:41,611
Creating a scope doesn't allow it on any application.

79
00:05:41,611 --> 00:05:47,277
Let's open Applications, edit Inventory Sync, and check inventory under Allowed Scopes.

80
00:05:47,731 --> 00:05:51,367
Saving stores the choice as part of the application.

81
00:05:51,367 --> 00:05:55,631
Inventory Sync is then allowed to request the inventory scope.

82
00:05:57,631 --> 00:06:02,330
Applications register the programs that use your site as an identity provider, and

83
00:06:02,330 --> 00:06:04,573
scopes define what they can request.

84
00:06:04,573 --> 00:06:05,961
Thanks for watching.

